---
description: A review finding is about the pull request head it was written on; a review the owner asks for is posted on the pull request
alwaysApply: true
---

# Review findings

A comment on a pull request is about that pull request's head. Read it against that head before calling the pull request ready to merge.

The commit an inline comment is attached to (`original_commit_id`) is the head when the review was submitted, which is not always the head the reviewer read. Compare it with the head the review's summary names, and check the comment's claim in the current head's file before changing anything. In V1c10 a re-review written on bd02746 attached its inline comments to the next head, a812cc3, where its R1 was already fixed (PR #241).

A reply that the API refuses with `422` and "user_id can only have one pending review per pull request" means that the account has a review it has not submitted. The agent runs on the owner's account, so that is a review the owner is writing: do not delete or submit it, tell the owner, and post the replies after it is submitted. In V1c10 three replies waited for the owner's review this way (PR #241).

A finding that asks for a change is still open when that head does not contain the change. Say so. A later pull request stacked above the head does not resolve it.

A finding that is tried and not adopted is answered with the measurement or the reason, on the pull request it was written on.

Before acting on a finding or declining it, check its claim against the code of that head: run the reproduction, or read the lines it names. A finding can be right about the reader's difficulty and wrong about the fact. A finding that says what a rule or the design says is checked the same way: read the sentence it names before changing it. In V1c9 the design review (R7) called a sentence of `testing.mdc` wrong; read again, the sentence was about the operational log's `server.started` and `service.started` lines and was right, so it stayed and named those lines (PR #233).

A fix that goes further than its finding keeps the two parts apart: the part the finding names, and the part the fixer added, which is checked in the code before it is written. In V1d15 the fix for C3 (a machine fingerprint is what the client declares) was carried from early returns over to trials and said too much there: a trial file holds the fingerprint and is checked against it. The read of the fixed text (E1) found it (PR #429).

The fixed text is read again by a reviewer before the PR is called ready, for a fix that makes a guard stronger too. In V1d23 the fix for K1 bound `chain.reset` to the first record of the earlier log file, and the read after it found F1: when only the write to disk failed, memory and disk disagreed, and an untouched log could be judged replaced (PR #441).

A finding that asks to change a decision the owner made is not declined by citing the rule that records the decision. Reply with what the decision was and what the finding shows, and ask the owner (`.cursor/rules/consent.mdc`).

A review the owner asks for is posted on the pull request, in the same turn, without waiting for an ask to post it. A review shown only in the conversation is not seen by anyone else. A re-review after fixes is posted the same way. An independent design review the agent runs itself (`.cursor/rules/design-docs.mdc`) is posted too: the gist of each finding, how it was fixed, and the owner's decisions, on the Issue or the PR, in the turn its fixes land. In the release threats the review stayed in the conversation, and the owner's review asked for it (PR #151). The same holds for every other review the agent runs itself, such as the accuracy, first-time reader, and Japanese reviews after the implementation: post the gist of each finding and how it was fixed or why it was not, on the Issue or the PR, before the PR is called ready. In PR #220 the body gave only the number of findings, and the owner's review asked for the record. A sentence that says all findings were fixed and names the worst one is not the record either: in PR #257 the design, accuracy, and first-time reader reviews went to the Issue as tables of findings and fixes, while the Japanese review got one sentence in the PR body, and the owner's review asked for its table.

When several reviewers each take one part of a scope, build the coverage table from what each report says it did not read, before the findings are verified. A part nobody read gets a reviewer of its own. In V1d the reviewer of the on-premises server did not read `LogVerifier.cs`, so the threat of a changed usage log (T15) was only partly covered; the table of threats against reviewers showed it, and one more reviewer read the log code (PR #334).

The independent reviewer of a workflow, or of code that reads untrusted input, is told to read it as an attacker: to look for the gaps around a new check, not only for a way through it. In PR #388 such a review found no way through the new check and a gap beside it: `image-test` unpacks an artifact it has not verified over the checkout. In PR #391 it found that a log file whose name was changed drops out of the verification without a word.

Before a subagent's report is posted where others can read it, search it for the local account name, network addresses, and the shapes of keys and tokens. A report quotes paths and command output of the machine it ran on. In V1d thirteen reports went to a public Issue after such a search, which found nothing (PR #334).

Before calling a PR ready, and whenever the owner asks for the next step, read the reviews and inline comments of every open PR (`gh pr view <n> --json reviews,comments` and `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/<n>/comments`).

```text
# BAD
Owner: #12 をレビューして下さい
Agent: writes the findings in the conversation, and asks whether to post them

# GOOD
Owner: #12 をレビューして下さい
Agent: posts the findings as a comment on the pull request, and gives the link and a summary in the conversation
```
